LICENCE: GNU General Public License, version 2, with a font embedding exception
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The GPL version 2 text is already in this directory as **GPL-2**, and it is the right text: it
was compared against the COPYING shipped in the font's own upstream package and is the same
licence, differing only in the FSF's postal address (they moved in 2005) and in the preamble
saying "Lesser" where the older printing says "Library" (the FSF renamed the LGPL in 1999).
Both are official FSF printings of GPL version 2.

The exception, quoted VERBATIM from the upstream README written by Valek Filippov — the same
person named in this font's own /Notice:

    The license for result is (of course) same as for original fonts,
    i.e. GPL with an exception that you can put these fonts in your own non-GPLed
    documents. (Looks like LGPL from my point of view =).

and, from the acknowledgements in that same README:

    Also I have to thank RMS for GPL and URW for releasing the fonts
    under it.


The copyright notice carried by the font files themselves
-----------------------------------------------------------

From the .pfb's `/Notice` (PostScript `\050`/`\051` escapes resolved to the parentheses they
encode) and repeated identically in the .afm's `Notice` line:

    Copyright (URW)++,Copyright 1999 by (URW)++ Design & Development;
    Cyrillic glyphs added by Valek Filippov (C) 2001-2002


How this was established (2026-07-30)
---------------------------------------

Three independent sources agree, and the dangerous alternative was ruled out.

1. **The upstream package's own COPYING is the GPL version 2 text.**
2. **The upstream README states the grant**, quoted above. It is written by Filippov, who is
   named in our file's `/Notice`, describing the licence of exactly the work he produced.
3. **Debian's copyright file for the same package** records: "Upstream Authors: URW++ Design &
   Development GmbH / Valek Filippov <frob@df.ru> et. al." and "you can redistribute it and/or
   modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License ... version 2 dated June, 1991",
   sourced from `ftp://ftp.gnome.ru/fonts/urw/release/` (Filippov's urw-fonts) and the
   Ghostscript GPL fonts.

**AFPL is ruled out.** That was the real risk — the Nimbus family has also circulated under the
Aladdin Free Public License, which is NOT a free licence and would have been a genuine problem
rather than a paperwork one. Every source above says GPL.

Packages examined (Debian pool / snapshot.debian.org):

    gsfonts_8.11+urwcyr1.0.7~pre44.orig.tar.gz  sha256 9f2a598998bf05e023546ac981aa2a857aa1635d2e0e3020a3c3004ad564dc00
    gsfonts_8.11+urwcyr1.0.7~pre44-4.5_all.deb  sha256 74fa211939138b63fbe065247719881a1a0869beccadd2c719c3f7b7f70ce1a0
    gsfonts_8.14+v8.11+urw.orig.tar.gz          sha1   858d88497c446bdc6598009938c64fefad70b219


Honest limitation: no byte-identical release was found
--------------------------------------------------------

The intended proof was to byte-match our `n019004l.pfb` against a package and adopt that
package's licence. **That match was not obtained**, and this file says so rather than implying a
stronger result than was reached.

What was found instead is that all three copies are the same work regenerated at different
dates, which explains the byte difference without weakening the licence conclusion:

    ours                       pfaedit,            2002-12-31   72,400 bytes   Notice: 2001-2002
    gsfonts 8.14+v8.11+urw     pfaedit,            2005-03-27   73,988 bytes   Notice: 2001-2002
    gsfonts urwcyr1.0.7~pre44  FontForge 20070723, 2007-08-02   70,730 bytes   Notice: 2001-2005

All three carry identical `/FontName` (`NimbusSanL-Bold`), `/FullName` (`Nimbus Sans L Bold`)
and `/version` (1.06), and name the same two rights holders. Ours is simply the OLDEST — a 2002
pfaedit generation predating Debian's surviving archive, whose earliest gsfonts upload is 2005.
The middle copy carries a `/Notice` string byte-identical to ours.

So the licence is established by provenance rather than by byte-identity: our file is a build of
Filippov's Cyrillized URW fonts, and that work is GPL-2 with the embedding exception. If a
2002-vintage `urwcyr` release ever surfaces, byte-matching it would close the last gap.


Compatibility with this project — GPLv3 code beside a GPLv2 font
------------------------------------------------------------------

Vortex Arena's own source is "GPL version 3 or any later version" (see COPYING at the repo
root). GPLv2-only and GPLv3 are **incompatible for forming a single derived work**, so this
deserves a straight answer rather than a shrug:

**It is fine as we ship it**, for three reasons that must all keep holding:

  * We redistribute the font **unmodified, as a data file**. The program does not link it or
    incorporate it; it loads it at runtime like any other asset. That is mere aggregation on a
    distribution medium, not a derived work, so the two licences never have to combine.
  * The **font embedding exception** is broader than we need. It exists precisely so that using
    the font in output does not pull the GPL onto that output.
  * GPL-2 permits verbatim redistribution provided the copyright notice and the licence travel
    with the copy. That is what this file and GPL-2 in this directory now accomplish, and it is
    what was missing before.

**What would break it:** modifying the font, or building it into the program such that the
result is a derived work of both. Either would put a GPLv2-only work and GPLv3 code into one
combined work, which cannot be licensed. If a font needs changing, replace it with one whose
terms permit that rather than editing this one.

Note also that the grant is version 2, **not** "or later" — Debian pins "version 2 dated June,
1991", and the upstream COPYING is the bare GPL-2 text with no "or any later version" clause.
Do not assume v2+.


Historical note: upstream Xonotic ships no notice for this font at all
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Checked against the upstream reference tree: Xonotic ships the identical four font packs with
identical file lists, and the only font licence anywhere in it is Xolonium's fonts/README.txt.
There is no notice for this font, nothing in Docs/, nothing in its COPYING. This gap was
inherited, not introduced here — but it was still a gap, and it is now closed on our side.


Provenance of this file
-----------------------

Written 2026-07-30. The quoted exception is a verbatim copy from the upstream README; the
copyright notice is reproduced from the .pfb's `/Notice` and confirmed against the .afm.
Nothing here is transcribed from memory or summarised from a web page. See data/licenses/FONTS
for the other three font packs.
